\title{Known versus Unresolved Cards: An Exact Calculus of Certainty, Fair Odds, and the Value of Information}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{23 August 2026}

\begin{abstract}
We develop an exact, finite, distribution-free calculus for prediction games that mix cards \emph{known with certainty} with cards that must be \emph{guessed}. The central object is a splitting principle: if $d$ cards in a finite menu are resolved and each pays one unit, and every remaining card is fair --- meaning it has zero mean under the ambient law --- then the expected total payoff is exactly $d$, irrespective of correlations among the cards and irrespective of the guessing rule. Uncertainty supplies no positive edge.

We instantiate the principle in four increasingly adversarial settings. For a uniformly random bijection between $u$ slots and $u$ cards and an arbitrary, not necessarily injective, calling strategy $g$, we prove the master slot formula $\E[\text{slot score}] = (w-\ell)/u + \ell$, hence the block value $(w-\ell)+\ell u$, and a \emph{rigidity theorem}: the block is edge-free for one strategy if and only if $w = \ell(1-u)$, i.e.\ exactly at the honest $(u-1):1$ quote, and then for all strategies. We record the \emph{counting anomaly}: naive unit scoring shows a spurious edge of exactly $+1$, for every strategy and every $u$. The mean score is strategy-invariant but the variance is not: we prove the exact \emph{collision formula} $\Var[\hits] = D(g)/(u(u-1))$, where $D(g)$ counts ordered pairs of slots receiving distinct calls. With per-card feedback and unit scoring the value jumps from $1$ to the harmonic number $H_u$, which is unbounded, yet at stagewise fair odds it remains exactly $0$ for every admissible feedback strategy: information changes the price, not the edge. Finally, the No-Free-Lunch theorem is exhibited as an instance of the splitting principle via a fixed-point-free label-flip involution, with a $k$-ary generalisation driven by the free action of $\mathbb{Z}/k\mathbb{Z}$; and no adaptive, stopped betting system on a fair $\pm 1$ sequence has nonzero expected gain, including the doubling system, which wins with probability $1-2^{-n}$ and gains exactly $0$.

All results are exact identities over the rationals on finite sample spaces: no asymptotics, no measure-theoretic limits, no independence assumptions.
\end{abstract}

