\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The question}
A prediction problem rarely presents itself as pure uncertainty. Typically some part of the answer is \emph{known} --- determined by counting, by constraint, by prior observation --- and the rest is genuinely open. It is tempting to believe that the open part, being open, is where the value lies: that a sufficiently clever allocation of guesses across the unknown region converts uncertainty into expected profit.

The results below say, in four different languages, that it does not. Priced honestly, the open part contributes exactly zero, and the value of the game is exactly the number of things already known.

What makes the statement worth proving carefully rather than waving at is the number of places where it \emph{appears} to fail. It appears to fail when the scoring rule is not a fair book (the counting anomaly, \S\ref{sec:anomaly}); when a high win probability is mistaken for a positive expectation (the doubling system, \S\ref{sec:betting}); when strategies with equal means but different variances are compared (the collision formula, \S\ref{sec:second}); and when the player acquires information (feedback, \S\ref{sec:feedback}). Each is resolved exactly, and the resolutions differ quantitatively.

\subsection{Contributions}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em]
\item A splitting theorem for arbitrary finite index sets and arbitrary correlation structures (\S\ref{sec:splitting}).
\item Factorial-free fibre counting for the symmetric group via transposition symmetry, and its consequences for the first two moments of a general calling strategy (\S\ref{sec:deck}--\S\ref{sec:second}).
\item A rigidity characterisation of fair odds, upgrading ``no edge'' from an observation to a definition.
\item An exact collision formula for the variance of a general strategy.
\item A dichotomy for feedback: unit-scoring value $H_u$, unbounded; fair-odds value $0$.
\item A proof of No Free Lunch by a fixed-point-free involution, its $\mathbb{Z}/k\mathbb{Z}$ generalisation, and a sharpness counterexample.
\item A finite-horizon optional stopping theorem and a quantitative resolution of the doubling paradox.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Notation}
All payoffs are rational. $\Omega$ denotes a nonempty finite sample space carrying the uniform law, and for $f : \Omega \to \Q$,
\[
\E[f] = \frac{1}{|\Omega|}\sum_{\omega \in \Omega} f(\omega), \qquad \Var[f] = \E[f^2] - \E[f]^2 .
\]
$H_n = \sum_{j=1}^{n} 1/j$ is the $n$-th harmonic number, $H_0 = 0$, and $\Sym_u$ is the symmetric group on $u$ letters.

\section{The abstract game}

\begin{definition}[Card]
A \emph{card} on $\Omega$ is a function $p : \Omega \to \Q$, interpreted as a payoff.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Resolved]
A card $p$ is \emph{resolved with value $c$} if $p(\omega) = c$ for every $\omega \in \Omega$: the predictor knows the card and collects $c$ in every state of the world.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Fair]
A card $p$ is \emph{fair} if $\E[p] = 0$: the odds offered exactly compensate the residual uncertainty.
\end{definition}

Two remarks on generality. First, fairness is a property of a single card's marginal, not of the joint law; nothing is assumed about dependence. Second, ``resolved'' is pointwise rather than almost-sure; on a finite uniform space the distinction is immaterial, and the pointwise form is what one verifies in practice.

\begin{lemma}[Linearity]\label{lem:linear}
$\E[f+g] = \E[f]+\E[g]$, $\E[cf] = c\,\E[f]$, $\E[c]=c$, and for any finite family $(f_i)_{i\in S}$ one has $\E\big[\sum_{i\in S} f_i\big] = \sum_{i \in S}\E[f_i]$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Immediate from the definition; the family version follows by induction on $|S|$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:resolved}
If $p$ is resolved with value $c$ then $\E[p]=c$.
\end{lemma}

\section{The splitting theorem}\label{sec:splitting}

\begin{theorem}[Splitting theorem, weighted form]\label{thm:split}
Let $\iota$ be a finite index set, $(p_i)_{i\in\iota}$ a family of cards on $\Omega$, $K \subseteq \iota$, and $(c_i)_{i \in K}$ rationals. Suppose $p_i$ is resolved with value $c_i$ for every $i \in K$, and fair for every $i \notin K$. Then
\[
\E\Big[\sum_{i\in\iota} p_i\Big] = \sum_{i\in K} c_i .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Lemma~\ref{lem:linear}, $\E[\sum_i p_i] = \sum_i \E[p_i]$. Split $\iota = K \sqcup (\iota\setminus K)$. On $K$, Lemma~\ref{lem:resolved} replaces each term by $c_i$; on $\iota \setminus K$ each term vanishes by fairness.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Headline form]\label{cor:headline}
If every card of $K$ is resolved with value $1$ and every card outside $K$ is fair, then $\E[\sum_i p_i] = |K|$. Writing $d = |K|$: \emph{$d$ cards predicted with certainty and any number of fair guesses are worth exactly $d$.}
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[No edge from uncertainty alone]\label{cor:noedge}
If every card is fair then $\E[\sum_i p_i]=0$, regardless of how the cards are correlated and how the guesses were chosen.
\end{corollary}

Corollary~\ref{cor:noedge} is the case $K = \emptyset$. It is \emph{not} a statement about independent bets: linearity of expectation is indifferent to dependence, and every application below involves heavily dependent cards.

\section{Shuffled decks: the blind game}\label{sec:deck}

\subsection{The model and two counting identities}
Let $\alpha$ be a finite set of size $u = |\alpha| \ge 1$ whose elements serve simultaneously as \emph{slots} and as \emph{cards}. The true arrangement is a uniformly random $\sigma \in \Sym_\alpha$, so $\Omega = \Sym_\alpha$ and $|\Omega| = u!$. A \emph{strategy} is an arbitrary function $g : \alpha \to \alpha$: in slot $i$ the predictor calls card $g(i)$. Non-injective strategies are permitted.

\begin{definition}
For $i,a \in \alpha$ set $F(i,a) = \{\sigma : \sigma(i)=a\}$, and for $i,j,a,b$ set $F_2(i,j,a,b) = \{\sigma : \sigma(i)=a,\ \sigma(j)=b\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[First counting identity]\label{thm:fib1}
$u\cdot|F(i,a)| = |\Sym_\alpha|$ for all $i,a \in \alpha$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
For $a,b \in \alpha$, the map $\sigma \mapsto \tau_{ab}\circ\sigma$, with $\tau_{ab}$ the transposition exchanging $a$ and $b$, is a self-inverse bijection $F(i,a)\to F(i,b)$. Hence all $u$ sets $F(i,a)$ have equal cardinality; they are pairwise disjoint and their union is $\Sym_\alpha$, since every $\sigma$ sends $i$ to exactly one card.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Second counting identity]\label{thm:fib2}
For $i\ne j$ and $a\ne b$, $\;(u-1)\cdot|F_2(i,j,a,b)| = |F(i,a)|$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Fix $i,a$ and work inside $F(i,a)$. For $b,b' \in \alpha\setminus\{a\}$, left composition with $\tau_{bb'}$ maps $F_2(i,j,a,b)$ bijectively onto $F_2(i,j,a,b')$ and preserves the constraint $\sigma(i)=a$, since $\tau_{bb'}$ fixes $a$. These $u-1$ classes partition $F(i,a)$, because $\sigma(j) \ne \sigma(i) = a$.
\end{proof}

Neither proof mentions a factorial. This is not affectation: the transposition-symmetry form generalises verbatim to $j$-fold constraints, which is the route to higher moments (\S\ref{sec:future}).

\subsection{The slot formula and the block value}

\begin{definition}
The \emph{slot score} with hit payoff $w$ and miss payoff $\ell$ is
\[
s_{w,\ell}(i,a;\sigma) = \begin{cases} w, & \sigma(i)=a,\\ \ell, & \text{otherwise.}\end{cases}
\]
The \emph{deck score} of a strategy $g$ is $S_{w,\ell}(g;\sigma) = \sum_{i\in\alpha}s_{w,\ell}(i,g(i);\sigma)$, and the \emph{hit count} is $\hits(g;\sigma) = \#\{i : \sigma(i)=g(i)\}$, so $S_{1,0}(g;\sigma) = \hits(g;\sigma)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Master slot formula]\label{thm:slot}
For every $i,a \in \alpha$,
\[
\E\big[s_{w,\ell}(i,a;\cdot)\big] = \frac{w-\ell}{u} + \ell .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Write $s_{w,\ell}(i,a;\sigma) = (w-\ell)\ind{\sigma(i)=a} + \ell$. Summing over $\sigma$ gives $(w-\ell)|F(i,a)| + \ell|\Sym_\alpha|$; divide by $|\Sym_\alpha|$ and use $|F(i,a)|/|\Sym_\alpha| = 1/u$ from Theorem~\ref{thm:fib1}.
\end{proof}

The right-hand side depends on neither $i$ nor $a$. Summing over the $u$ slots:

\begin{theorem}[Block value]\label{thm:block}
For every strategy $g$, $\;\E[S_{w,\ell}(g;\cdot)] = (w-\ell) + \ell u$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[Strategy invariance of the mean]\label{cor:mean}
$\E[\hits(g;\cdot)] = 1$ for every $g$; equivalently $\sum_\sigma \hits(g;\sigma) = |\Sym_\alpha|$.
\end{corollary}

For $g = \mathrm{id}$ this is the classical fact that a uniform random permutation has one fixed point in expectation; the content here is that the same holds for \emph{every} $g$, including constant ones.

\subsection{Rigidity of fair odds}

\begin{theorem}[Rigidity]\label{thm:rigid}
For any strategy $g$, $\;\E[S_{w,\ell}(g;\cdot)] = 0 \iff w = \ell(1-u)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:block} the expectation is $(w-\ell)+\ell u$, which vanishes iff $w = \ell - \ell u$.
\end{proof}

Because the criterion does not involve $g$, the block is edge-free for \emph{one} strategy exactly when it is edge-free for \emph{all}. Setting $\ell = -1$ gives $w = u-1$: the $(u-1):1$ quote, the honest price when $u$ candidates are equally likely. Fairness is thus characterised rather than assumed. Conversely, any $(w,\ell)$ with $w \ne \ell(1-u)$ endows every strategy with the same nonzero edge $(w-\ell)+\ell u$: the edge belongs to the book, not to the player.

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:fair}
$\E[S_{u-1,-1}(g;\cdot)] = 0$ for every $g$.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{The full deck and the counting anomaly}\label{sec:anomaly}

\begin{definition}
The \emph{deck game} with $d$ resolved cards has menu indexed by $\{1,\dots,d\}\sqcup\alpha$, where each resolved card pays the constant $1$ and the card at slot $i \in \alpha$ pays $s_{u-1,-1}(i,g(i);\sigma)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Known versus unresolved cards]\label{thm:headline}
For every $d \ge 0$ and every strategy $g$, the expected total payoff of the deck game is exactly $d$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:split} with $K$ the copy of $\{1,\dots,d\}$. Those cards are resolved with value $1$ by construction; each card of $\alpha$ is fair because Theorem~\ref{thm:slot} with $w=u-1$, $\ell=-1$ gives $\E[s] = \big((u-1)+1\big)/u - 1 = 0$ slotwise.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Counting anomaly]\label{thm:count}
Under unit scoring the same deck yields $\E\big[d + S_{1,0}(g;\cdot)\big] = d+1$, for every $d$, every $g$, and every $u \ge 1$.
\end{theorem}

The discrepancy between Theorems~\ref{thm:headline} and~\ref{thm:count} is exactly one card, uniformly in every parameter. It is entirely a pricing artefact: unit scoring rewards hits without charging for misses, and by Theorem~\ref{thm:rigid} it is not a fair book unless $u=1$. Any inference of an ``edge from uncertainty'' calibrated on hit counts rather than on a fair book will report exactly this $+1$, no matter how large the unresolved region.

\section{Second moments: what strategy does control}\label{sec:second}

\subsection{The injective and constant extremes}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:inj}
If $u \ge 2$ and $g$ is injective, then $\sum_\sigma \hits(g;\sigma)^2 = 2|\Sym_\alpha|$, i.e.\ $\E[\hits^2]=2$ and $\Var[\hits]=1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:const}
If $g \equiv a$ is constant then $\hits(g;\sigma)=1$ for every $\sigma$; hence $\E[\hits^2]=1$ and $\Var[\hits]=0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\{i : \sigma(i)=a\} = \{\sigma^{-1}(a)\}$ is a singleton.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:const} is a pleasing degenerate case: naming the same card in every slot yields exactly one correct call with probability one. The score is deterministic, not merely mean-one.

\begin{corollary}[Second-moment dichotomy]
For $u \ge 2$ and any $a \in \alpha$, the identity strategy and the constant-$a$ strategy have equal means and unequal variances:
\[
\E[\hits(\mathrm{id})] = \E[\hits(a)] = 1, \qquad \Var[\hits(\mathrm{id})] = 1 \ne 0 = \Var[\hits(a)].
\]
\end{corollary}

\subsection{The collision formula}

\begin{definition}
The \emph{collision profile} of $g$ is the number of ordered pairs of slots receiving distinct calls,
\[
D(g) = \#\{(i,j)\in\alpha\times\alpha : g(i)\ne g(j)\} = \sum_{i\in\alpha}\#\{j : g(i)\ne g(j)\}.
\]
Thus $D(g) = u(u-1)$ if $g$ is injective and $D(g)=0$ if $g$ is constant.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Exact second moment]\label{thm:secmom}
For every strategy $g$ on a block of size $u$,
\[
u(u-1)\sum_\sigma \hits(g;\sigma)^2 = \big(u(u-1) + D(g)\big)\,|\Sym_\alpha| .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Expand $\hits^2 = \sum_i\sum_j \ind{\sigma(i)=g(i)}\ind{\sigma(j)=g(j)}$ and sum over $\sigma$. Diagonal terms contribute $\sum_i |F(i,g(i))| = |\Sym_\alpha|$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:fib1}. For $i \ne j$ the term is $|F_2(i,j,g(i),g(j))|$, which vanishes when $g(i)=g(j)$ (a permutation cannot place one card in two slots) and equals $|F(i,g(i))|/(u-1) = |\Sym_\alpha|/(u(u-1))$ when $g(i)\ne g(j)$, by Theorem~\ref{thm:fib2}. Exactly $D(g)$ off-diagonal pairs survive.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Collision formula for the variance]\label{thm:collision}
For $u \ge 2$ and every strategy $g$,
\[
\Var\big[\hits(g;\cdot)\big] = \frac{D(g)}{u(u-1)} \in [0,1].
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Divide Theorem~\ref{thm:secmom} by $u(u-1)|\Sym_\alpha|$ to get $\E[\hits^2] = 1 + D(g)/(u(u-1))$, then subtract $\E[\hits]^2 = 1$ from Corollary~\ref{cor:mean}.
\end{proof}

Theorems~\ref{thm:inj} and~\ref{thm:const} are the endpoints $D=u(u-1)$ and $D=0$. The formula is exact for every strategy and makes precise the boundary of strategy invariance: \emph{the first moment cannot see the strategy at all; the second moment sees precisely and only its pattern of repeated calls, normalised by the number of ordered slot pairs.}

The practical reading is that in a fairly priced game, choosing a strategy is choosing a risk profile. Maximal hedging (a constant call) delivers certainty of exactly one hit; maximal spread (an injective call) delivers a Poisson-like fluctuation of variance $1$; intermediate patterns interpolate linearly in $D(g)$.

\section{Feedback: the value of information}\label{sec:feedback}

\subsection{The sequential model}
In the \emph{feedback game} the predictor plays through the unresolved block one card at a time, and after each call the card is revealed. The only relevant state is the set $S$ of still-unseen cards.

\begin{definition}
A \emph{feedback strategy} is a map $g$ from finite subsets of $\alpha$ to $\alpha$. It is \emph{admissible} if $g(S) \in S$ for every nonempty $S$: it never names a card already seen.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
Given payoff schedules $\mathrm{hit},\mathrm{miss} : \mathbb{N}\to\Q$ depending on the number of live cards, the value $V(S)$ on live set $S$ is defined by $V(\emptyset)=0$ and, for $S \ne \emptyset$,
\[
V(S) = \frac{1}{|S|}\sum_{a\in S}\Big[\big(\mathrm{hit}(|S|)\ \text{if } g(S)=a,\ \text{else } \mathrm{miss}(|S|)\big) + V(S\setminus\{a\})\Big].
\]
The recursion terminates since $|S\setminus\{a\}| < |S|$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[One-stage recursion]\label{lem:step}
If $S \ne \emptyset$ and $g(S)\in S$, then
\[
V(S) = \frac{|S|\,\mathrm{miss}(|S|) + \big(\mathrm{hit}(|S|)-\mathrm{miss}(|S|)\big) + \sum_{a\in S}V(S\setminus\{a\})}{|S|}.
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Write the bracketed payoff as $\mathrm{miss}(|S|) + \big(\mathrm{hit}(|S|)-\mathrm{miss}(|S|)\big)\ind{g(S)=a}$ and use that exactly one $a\in S$ satisfies $g(S)=a$, by admissibility.
\end{proof}

Admissibility is exactly the hypothesis making the indicator sum to $1$; for an inadmissible call it sums to $0$ and the player scores a certain miss.

\subsection{The unit-scoring value is harmonic}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:harm}
With $\mathrm{hit}\equiv 1$ and $\mathrm{miss}\equiv 0$, every admissible feedback strategy satisfies $V(S) = H_{|S|}$ for every finite $S$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Strong induction on $|S| = m$. Lemma~\ref{lem:step} gives $V(S) = \big(1 + \sum_{a\in S}V(S\setminus\{a\})\big)/m$. Each erased set has $m-1$ elements, so by induction the sum is $m H_{m-1}$, whence $V(S) = 1/m + H_{m-1} = H_m$.
\end{proof}

The value is thus independent of the admissible strategy: cleverness is irrelevant provided one never re-calls a seen card. Compare Corollary~\ref{cor:mean}: a blind pass scores exactly $1$.

\begin{theorem}[Oresme bound]\label{thm:oresme}
$1 + n/2 \le H_{2^n}$ for every $n \ge 0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction, using $\sum_{j=m+1}^{2m}1/j \ge m\cdot\frac{1}{2m} = \frac12$ for $m \ge 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Unbounded advantage]\label{cor:unbounded}
For every $C \in \Q$ and every admissible feedback strategy there is a finite live set $S$ with $V(S) > C$ under unit scoring, while every blind strategy scores exactly $1$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Blind versus informed]
For $u \ge 2$, every blind strategy $g$ and every admissible feedback strategy, $\E[\hits(g;\cdot)] = 1 < H_u$, since $H_2 = 3/2$ and $H$ is monotone.
\end{corollary}

So feedback is worth exactly $H_u - 1 \sim \log u$ additional correct calls.

\subsection{Fair odds are information-proof}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:infoproof}
With the stagewise fair schedule $\mathrm{hit}(m) = m-1$ and $\mathrm{miss}(m) = -1$, every admissible feedback strategy satisfies $V(S) = 0$ for every finite $S$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Strong induction on $|S| = m \ge 1$. Lemma~\ref{lem:step} gives
\[
V(S) = \frac{m(-1) + \big((m-1)-(-1)\big) + \sum_{a\in S}V(S\setminus\{a\})}{m} = \frac{-m+m+0}{m} = 0,
\]
using $V(S\setminus\{a\}) = 0$ for each of the $m$ successors.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
For nonempty finite $\alpha$, the blind fair-odds book and the sequential fair-odds book are both worth exactly $0$; and $d$ resolved cards plus a feedback-played unresolved block at stagewise fair odds is worth exactly $d$.
\end{corollary}

The pair (Theorem~\ref{thm:harm}, Theorem~\ref{thm:infoproof}) is the sharpest statement in the paper. Information is genuinely valuable --- worth an unbounded number of cards --- but only against a static, mispriced book. Against a book repricing at each stage to the honest $(m-1):1$ given $m$ live candidates, information is worth exactly nothing. What information buys is not an edge but a change in the fair price; it becomes an edge only when someone fails to update.

\section{The learning-theoretic incarnation}\label{sec:nfl}

\subsection{Setup}
Let $X$ be a finite domain. A \emph{target} is a function $f : X \to \{\text{false},\text{true}\}$, drawn uniformly from the $2^{|X|}$ possibilities. A \emph{learner} is a map $L$ from targets to hypotheses $L(f) : X \to \{\text{false},\text{true}\}$. Fix a training set $T \subseteq X$ and impose:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em]
\item \textbf{(Blindness)} $L(f) = L(f')$ whenever $f$ and $f'$ agree on $T$;
\item \textbf{(Consistency)} $L(f)(y) = f(y)$ for every $y \in T$ and every $f$.
\end{itemize}
Score each point $y$ by $+1$ if $L(f)(y) = f(y)$ and $-1$ otherwise: exactly the fair $(k-1):1$ book at $k=2$.

\subsection{The flip involution}

\begin{definition}
For $x \in X$, let $\flip_x(f)$ agree with $f$ everywhere except at $x$, where it takes the opposite value.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}
$\flip_x$ is a fixed-point-free involution of target space, and $\flip_x(f)$ agrees with $f$ on $X\setminus\{x\}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Off-training cards are fair]\label{thm:offfair}
If $x \notin T$ and $L$ is blind, then $\sum_f \big(\ind{L(f)(x)=f(x)} - \ind{L(f)(x)\ne f(x)}\big) = 0$; that is, the $\pm1$ score at $x$ has mean zero.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Since $x \notin T$, the targets $f$ and $\flip_x(f)$ agree on $T$, so blindness gives $L(\flip_x(f)) = L(f)$: the \emph{prediction} at $x$ is unchanged while the \emph{truth} at $x$ is flipped, so the score at $x$ is negated. As $\flip_x$ is a fixed-point-free involution it pairs target space into two-element orbits on which the scores cancel.
\end{proof}

\subsection{No Free Lunch}

\begin{theorem}[No Free Lunch]\label{thm:nfl}
For every blind, consistent learner $L$, the expected total $\pm1$ score over $X$ is exactly $|T|$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:split} with cards indexed by $X$: for $y \in T$ consistency makes the card resolved with value $1$; for $y \notin T$, Theorem~\ref{thm:offfair} makes it fair.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Chance level off-sample]
The expected number of correctly predicted points is $|T| + \tfrac{|X|-|T|}{2} = \tfrac{|T|+|X|}{2}$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
The $\{0,1\}$ accuracy indicator equals $\tfrac12(1 + \text{the } \pm1 \text{ score})$; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:nfl} and linearity.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sharpness]
Blindness cannot be dropped: with $T = \emptyset$, the ``learner'' $L(f) = f$ is vacuously consistent and achieves expected $\pm1$ score $|X| \ne 0 = |T|$.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{The $k$-ary generalisation}
Let $k \ge 1$ and let labels take values in $\mathbb{Z}/k\mathbb{Z}$. Price a card at fair odds $(k-1):1$: score $k-1$ for a correct label and $-1$ otherwise. Replace the flip by $\shift_{x,t}(f)$, which adds $t \in \mathbb{Z}/k\mathbb{Z}$ to the label at $x$ and leaves all other labels alone.

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:kary1}
If $x \notin T$ and $L$ is blind, then $\sum_f \big((k-1)\ind{L(f)(x)=f(x)} - \ind{L(f)(x)\ne f(x)}\big) = 0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The maps $\{\shift_{x,t}\}_t$ form a free action of $\mathbb{Z}/k\mathbb{Z}$ whose orbits have size exactly $k$, all members agreeing off $x$ and taking all $k$ values at $x$. Blindness makes the prediction at $x$ constant along an orbit, so exactly one member is a hit, scoring $k-1$, and the other $k-1$ each score $-1$; each orbit contributes $0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[$k$-ary No Free Lunch]
For every blind, consistent learner over a $k$-letter alphabet, the expected total fair-odds score is exactly $(k-1)|T|$.
\end{theorem}

The orbit argument generalises the involution precisely: at $k=2$ the group $\mathbb{Z}/2\mathbb{Z}$ acts by the flip and $(k-1):1$ reduces to $\pm1$. The interpretation is unchanged: training points are known cards paying the full $k-1$; off-training points are unresolved cards paying zero on average.

\begin{remark}
The theorem does not say learning is futile; it says the uniform prior over targets is exactly an unresolved block. All generalisation performance of a working algorithm is borrowed from the fact that empirical targets are not uniformly distributed --- from structure, from smoothness, from an inductive bias that is itself a form of resolved information. In the vocabulary of this paper, real problems have $d > |T|$.
\end{remark}

\section{No betting system beats a fair book}\label{sec:betting}

\subsection{Adaptive stakes}
Let the player observe a sequence of independent fair $\pm1$ tosses, encoded as a finite history $h$. A \emph{betting system} is an arbitrary function $\mathrm{stake}$ from histories to $\Q$: before the next toss the player stakes $\mathrm{stake}(h)$, winning that amount on heads and losing it on tails. Negative stakes are allowed (switching sides), unbounded stakes are allowed, and a stake of $0$ encodes ``quit'', so optional stopping is subsumed.

\begin{definition}
The expected net gain over a horizon of $n$ further tosses from history $h$ is defined by $G(0,h)=0$ and
\[
G(n+1,h) = \tfrac12\Big[\big(\mathrm{stake}(h) + G(n, h\mathrm{H})\big) + \big(-\mathrm{stake}(h) + G(n, h\mathrm{T})\big)\Big].
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[No betting system]\label{thm:nobet}
$G(n,h)=0$ for every system, every $n$, and every $h$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Induction on $n$. The base case is by definition. For the step, the two occurrences of $\mathrm{stake}(h)$ cancel and both continuation values vanish by the inductive hypothesis, which applies to \emph{every} history --- including the two extensions.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Optional stopping]
For any stopping rule, betting $\mathrm{bet}(h)$ until the rule fires and $0$ thereafter yields expected gain $0$ over every finite horizon.
\end{corollary}

Theorem~\ref{thm:nobet} is the finite-horizon optional stopping theorem, obtained without measure theory: the sample space at each horizon is finite and the argument is a two-line induction. Its force is that adaptivity is fully general.

\subsection{The doubling paradox}

\begin{definition}
The \emph{doubling system} stakes $1$; after $k$ consecutive losses it stakes $2^k$; on the first win it stops.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:geom}
$2^k - \sum_{j=0}^{k-1}2^j = 1$ for every $k \ge 0$.
\end{lemma}

Thus after $k$ losses the player is down $2^k-1$, stakes $2^k$, and a win leaves her exactly one unit ahead.

\begin{definition}
Over a horizon of $n$ tosses the net gain of the doubling system is
\[
D_n(w) = \begin{cases} -(2^n-1), & \text{if every toss in } w \text{ is a loss},\\ +1, & \text{otherwise.}\end{cases}
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:doubzero}
$\sum_{w}D_n(w) = 0$ over the $2^n$ outcomes; equivalently $\E[D_n]=0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Exactly one of the $2^n$ outcomes is all-losses, so the sum is $(2^n-1)\cdot 1 - (2^n-1) = 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}
$\Prob[D_n > 0] = 1 - 2^{-n}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Doubling paradox, resolved]
For every $\varepsilon > 0$ there is an $n$ with $\E[D_n]=0$ and $\Prob[D_n>0] > 1-\varepsilon$.
\end{theorem}

The resolution is quantitative and complete: the rare loss $-(2^n-1)$ is exactly as large as the frequent gain $+1$ is likely. \emph{A high win rate is not an edge}, and Theorem~\ref{thm:nobet} says the same in full generality: any system that wins small amounts almost always must finance them with a rare loss of exactly compensating expected magnitude.

\section{Algorithms}

Three routines suffice to verify every identity above on concrete instances.

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{A. Exact block enumeration.} Enumerate $\Sym_u$ and accumulate the exact rational moments of $\hits(g;\cdot)$ for a given $g$. Cost $O(u!\cdot u)$ time and $O(u)$ space; feasible to $u \approx 9$ and sufficient to confirm the slot formula, block value, counting anomaly and collision formula exactly, with no floating-point error, since all quantities are rational with denominator dividing $u!$.

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{B. Collision profile.} Compute $D(g) = \sum_i \#\{j : g(i)\ne g(j)\}$ in $O(u)$ time by tallying call multiplicities: if the distinct called cards have multiplicities $m_1,\dots,m_r$ with $\sum_t m_t = u$, then $D(g) = u^2 - \sum_t m_t^2$. With Theorem~\ref{thm:collision} this evaluates the exact variance in linear time, replacing an $O(u!)$ enumeration.

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{C. Feedback recursion.} Evaluate $V(S)$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:step}. Naively $O(u!)$, but under any admissible strategy the value depends on $S$ only through $|S|$, collapsing to the scalar iteration
\[
V(m) = \frac{m\,\mathrm{miss}(m) + \mathrm{hit}(m)-\mathrm{miss}(m)}{m} + V(m-1)
\]
in $O(u)$ operations. With $\mathrm{hit}\equiv1$, $\mathrm{miss}\equiv0$ this is $V(m) = 1/m + V(m-1) = H_m$; with $\mathrm{hit}(m)=m-1$, $\mathrm{miss}(m)=-1$ it is $V(m) = V(m-1) = 0$.

\section{Applications}

\noindent\textbf{Calibration of predictive scores.} Theorem~\ref{thm:rigid} gives an operational test for whether a scoring rule is a fair book: compute $(w-\ell)+\ell u$. If nonzero, every submission --- including a degenerate one --- earns the same spurious edge, and the leaderboard measures the rule rather than the predictors. Theorem~\ref{thm:count} quantifies the commonest failure mode, accuracy-style scoring, at exactly one unit.

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Card counting and information pricing.} Theorem~\ref{thm:headline} says the value of tracked information is exactly the number of cards it resolves, and Theorem~\ref{thm:infoproof} says this persists when information arrives sequentially, provided the counterparty reprices. Real advantage therefore requires a \emph{pricing lag}, and Corollary~\ref{cor:unbounded} bounds what that lag is worth in the unit-scoring caricature: $H_u - 1$.

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Benchmark design in learning.} The uniform-target average accuracy of any blind consistent learner is $(|T|+|X|)/2$. A benchmark averaging over unstructured targets therefore cannot distinguish algorithms at all; observed differences on real benchmarks measure the match between an algorithm's inductive bias and the structure of the target distribution, not a generic learning capability.

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Risk shaping under a fair book.} Theorem~\ref{thm:collision} gives an exact linear-time knob: to attain variance $v \in [0,1]$, choose a calling pattern with $D(g) = v\,u(u-1)$, e.g.\ by partitioning slots into groups sharing a call. The mean is unaffected.

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Money management.} Theorem~\ref{thm:nobet} and the doubling analysis are the exact statements behind the standard warning about progression systems: any staking scheme, however adaptive, has zero expected gain at fair odds, and the systems that look best in short samples are precisely those whose losses are rarest and largest.

\section{Discussion}

Three themes recur.

\emph{Linearity is enough, and it is indifferent to dependence.} Every application here involves strongly dependent cards --- a permutation forbids repeated placements, a target's labels are shared by all points, a betting history conditions all future stakes. None of the proofs needs independence, because none needs anything beyond linearity of expectation and cardwise fairness. This is why the splitting theorem can be stated once and instantiated four times.

\emph{``No edge'' is a characterisation of fairness, not a corollary of it.} Theorem~\ref{thm:rigid} is the pivot: $w = \ell(1-u)$ is forced by demanding zero expectation for a single strategy, and it then delivers zero expectation for all. The same rigidity holds stagewise in the feedback game.

\emph{The first moment is blind; the second is not.} Corollary~\ref{cor:mean} and Theorem~\ref{thm:collision} delimit exactly how much of a strategy is visible to the payoff distribution: nothing in the mean, and precisely the equivalence relation ``same call'' in the variance, through $D(g)$ and nothing else. The quantity a player cares about (return) is beyond her control; the quantity she can control (risk) is described by one combinatorial statistic computable in linear time.

A limitation worth stating plainly: everything here is exact and finite, and deliberately avoids limits. Theorem~\ref{thm:nobet} is a finite-horizon statement, and the doubling paradox is resolved \emph{within} a finite horizon --- the honest setting, since the usual resolution by appeal to infinite time obscures that even at horizon $n$ the expectation is already exactly zero.

\section{Future directions}\label{sec:future}

\begin{conjecture}[Bell-number moment hierarchy]
For an injective strategy $g$ on an unresolved block of size $u$ and every $k \le u$,
\[
\E\big[\hits(g;\cdot)^k\big] = B_k,
\]
the $k$-th Bell number. For non-injective $g$ the moments are strictly smaller for $k \ge 2$, and the whole moment sequence is determined by the set-partition statistics of the fibres of $g$.
\end{conjecture}

The two counting identities of \S\ref{sec:deck} are the first two rungs of a ladder: the number of permutations pinned on a $j$-element set of slots ought to be $|\Sym_u|/(u)_j$ with $(u)_j = u(u-1)\cdots(u-j+1)$, by the same transposition-symmetry argument applied $j$ times. Expanding $\hits^k$ over $k$-tuples of slots and grouping by the induced partition would then give the Bell numbers, since each set partition of $\{1,\dots,k\}$ contributes exactly $1$. This would say the hit count of a blind injective strategy is \emph{exactly} Poisson$(1)$ in all moments up to order $u$, not merely asymptotically. The missing ingredient is bookkeeping over set partitions, not new combinatorics.

\begin{conjecture}[Sequential rigidity of fair odds]
In the feedback game, a history-dependent pricing $(w_S,\ell_S)$ makes the game a zero-expectation bet for \emph{every} admissible strategy if and only if $w_S = \ell_S(1-|S|)$ at every reachable state $S$.
\end{conjecture}

The forward direction is Theorem~\ref{thm:infoproof} with general $\ell_S$; the converse requires exhibiting, at each state where the identity fails, a strategy detecting the discrepancy, which Lemma~\ref{lem:step} should supply by backward induction from the deepest violating state.

A third, more speculative direction: $D(g)$ is a single scalar summarising the fibre structure of $g$, and the Bell hierarchy suggests the whole moment sequence is a function of the fibre \emph{partition} of $g$, i.e.\ of an integer partition of $u$. Identifying which partitions are extremal for the $k$-th moment --- a discrete optimisation over Young diagrams --- would completely describe the achievable risk profiles in a fair blind prediction game.

\section{Conclusion}

We have given an exact calculus for prediction games mixing certainty with uncertainty, and shown that the value of such a game is precisely the number of cards known with certainty. The result is stable under everything one might hope would break it: arbitrary correlation, arbitrary and even degenerate strategies, sequential information, and unboundedly adaptive staking. It fails only when the book is mispriced, and then by an amount that is a property of the book: exactly $+1$ for accuracy-style scoring in the blind game, exactly $H_u-1$ in the feedback game. Where the player retains genuine freedom is in the second moment, where the exact collision formula $\Var = D(g)/(u(u-1))$ shows she controls the risk of a fairly priced game completely, and its return not at all.

